(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-08"] = {"6.1":{"s":"A function f from a set A to a set B gives each input in A exactly one output in B. The set A of inputs is the domain, which is the set of x-values for which f(x) is defined. B is the co-domain. The range is the set of outputs that really occur, and it can be smaller than B. On a graph, the domain lies along the x-axis and the range along the y-axis.","w":["Each input must have exactly one output: a vertical line cuts the graph of a function at most once.","Range and co-domain are different: the range is only the values actually reached."]},"6.2":{"s":"A function is one-one if different inputs always give different outputs. It is onto if every element of the co-domain is an output of some input. A function that is both is a bijection, and only a bijection has an inverse. If f takes A to B and g takes B to C, the composite function g∘f applies f first and then g. The inverse of f takes each output back to its input.","f":[{"x":"f(x_1) = f(x_2) => x_1 = x_2","n":"Test for a one-one function f. For onto: every y in the co-domain equals f(x) for some x in the domain.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.128"},{"x":"(g∘f)(x) = g(f(x))","n":"Composite function: first f, then g.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.128"}],"w":["In g∘f the function nearest to x acts first: f first, then g. And g∘f is usually not the same as f∘g.","A function has an inverse only if it is both one-one and onto."]},"6.3":{"s":"A function can be shown in several ways: as a formula, as a table of inputs and outputs, as a set of ordered pairs, as an arrow diagram between two sets, or as a graph in the plane. All of these carry the same information. A graph is the most useful way to see the domain and range, the increasing and decreasing parts, and whether the function is one-one.","w":["A graph is a function only if no vertical line meets it twice, and it is one-one if no horizontal line meets it twice."]},"6.4":{"s":"Some functions are used again and again. A constant function gives one fixed value. The identity function returns its input. A polynomial function is built from whole powers of x; the linear, quadratic and cubic functions are the common ones. The square root function is defined only for non-negative x. A rational function is a ratio of polynomials and excludes the zeros of the denominator. The exponential and logarithmic functions have fixed domains and ranges.","f":[{"x":"f(x) = sqrt(x)","n":"Domain [0, ∞), range [0, ∞).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = p(x)/q(x)","n":"Rational function: domain is all real x except where q(x) = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = a^x, a > 1","n":"Exponential function: domain R, range (0, ∞).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = log_a x, a > 1","n":"Logarithmic function: domain (0, ∞), range R.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"}],"w":["The domain of a square root or a logarithm excludes the values that make the inside negative (or zero, for a log).","The exponential never reaches 0 or a negative value, so its range is (0, ∞), not R."]},"6.5":{"s":"A piece-wise function uses different rules on different parts of its domain, so each input must be put into the rule for its own part. Special functions of this kind are the absolute value or modulus, which is never negative, the greatest integer function that gives the whole number just below or at x, the fractional part function, and the signum function that returns −1, 0 or 1 according to the sign of x.","f":[{"x":"f(x) = |x|","n":"Modulus (absolute) function: domain R, range [0, ∞).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"},{"x":"f(x) = sgn(x)","n":"Signum function: domain R, range {-1, 0, 1}.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.129"}],"w":["Check which part of the domain the input belongs to before using a rule; at the break point use the rule that includes it.","[x] of a negative number goes down: [−2.3] = −3, not −2."]}};
